WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.759 --> 00:00:12.440
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good morning, and welcome
this morning to the meeting of the Board of

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:19.070
NHS England. As always, I need to emphasise
that this is a meeting of the Board in public,

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:24.119
not a public meeting; however, it is a meeting
at which the Board will be assuring itself

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:30.260
of the work of NHS England and the broader
NHS, and also taking decisions which affect

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:37.809
the way in which the NHS operates. I would
also point out that the NHS Board here works

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:42.030
in many different ways; we work through a
number of committees, and reports of those

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:47.109
will come to the Board this morning. We also
will, at the end of this meeting, adjourn

00:00:47.109 --> 00:00:53.399
into a private meeting, in which we will consider
business that is necessary to transact in

00:00:53.399 --> 00:01:00.629
a private forum. We have also, throughout
our existence, had a strategy of working together

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:05.549
to develop our ideas, early-stage policies
and strategies in something that we call the

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:09.631
Development Section, so we will be proceeding
into one of those this afternoon when, as

00:01:09.631 --> 00:01:14.810
a unitary board, everybody sits around the
table and focuses on some key issues and deep

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.990
dives. For example, this afternoon, we will
be thinking about the Five Year Forward View

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:27.810
and around the operating model and supported
delivery of the STPs.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.200
So far as the formal business this morning
is concerned, I have no apologies for absence;

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:38.840
we have a full turn-out. Declarations of interest:
every member of the Board has in front of

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:44.990
them a tabled standing list of interests.
May I just check whether there are any interests

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:49.390
not on that list which anybody would wish
to draw attention to, in relation to any item

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.840
on the agenda?
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: It is already on the list,

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.720
but I just want to point out for the cancer
discussion: I am a director/trustee for Macmillan

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.140
Cancer Support.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good, thank you. Any others?

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:08.590
No? Thank you very much. I would also urge
if there are any amendments that you would

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:12.830
wish to make to the tabled list, could you
please drop them to Lesley so that she can

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:18.219
maintain an updated version? I would particularly
like this morning to welcome Cally Palmer

00:02:18.219 --> 00:02:23.530
who joins us, and will be speaking to the
paper on Cancer Strategy. Cally is our National

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.190
Cancer Director, so you are very welcome.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.680
Let me please then invite the Board to approve

00:02:47.689 --> 00:02:54.680
the minutes of the last meeting, and I have
not been notified of any matters arising that

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:59.680
are not otherwise on the agenda, so may I
proceed on that basis? Thank you very much.

00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:04.189
The first item on the agenda
is the Chairman’s Report. Just a few things

00:03:04.189 --> 00:03:11.180
that I would like to report. The first is
to record that this is a meeting which normally

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.549
is a meeting that is packed full of business,
because normally at this time of the year

00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:20.249
we are looking at the allocations and we are
looking at planning and operational guidance.

00:03:20.249 --> 00:03:23.969
What we have actually done, of course, is
to cancel the November meeting and have a

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:28.230
lighter agenda today, largely because much
of this business has been taken forward in

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:33.309
time, so that we are now three months ahead
on planning and operational guidance, and

00:03:33.309 --> 00:03:37.450
the contracting round is well advanced; others
will talk to us about that today. That means

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:43.359
that our last meeting was actually on 29th
September, but in between we had the AGM on

00:03:43.359 --> 00:03:49.680
25th October, at which it was possible for
us not only to reflect upon our Annual Report,

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.439
but also to draw attention to a number of
areas of priority on which we are focused,

00:03:55.439 --> 00:04:00.269
and our achievements for the past year and
our ambitions for the next year.

00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:05.879
The other activity which I have personally,
on behalf of the Board, been involved with

00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:11.230
includes various speaking engagements, which
I do not need to go into detail on, and liaisons

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:15.879
with stakeholders. One particular one that
I would mention is a visit to the Sheffield

00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:23.910
Test Bed on 20th October. As you know, we
are involved in experimenting with a number

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:30.510
of medical technologies, and giving an opportunity
for the developers to try them out in a real-world

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:35.640
environment, in households in Sheffield. I
think here there is some exciting potential

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:41.460
for development that could help and affect
our self-care programmes; not only medical

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:47.630
devices, but also the use of data to monitor
the condition of patients.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:52.200
The second issue would be just to report to
the Board that, although our November meeting

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:58.640
did not take place, we did take the opportunity
to confer awards on a group of outstanding

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:04.670
NHS England staff, the Everyone Counts awards.
I just want to stress how important it is

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:11.090
that so many of our staff do go the extra
mile for us and do things often behind the

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:15.560
scenes; I think one of the expressions that
came through so often in the citations was

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:20.120
that these were people who are self-effacing,
unobtrusive, they just got on and did stuff.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.650
I would like us formally today to commend
that.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:30.230
Also, on your behalf, I have attended a meeting
of the Board of NHS Improvement. I think it

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:35.340
is very important that we are not only working
together very closely at the leadership level,

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:40.150
but are seen to be working closely together,
and it gave me an opportunity to understand

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:45.610
their challenges; in particular, how they
could approach the deficit position in the

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:50.190
provider sector, and the steps that they were
taking.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:55.150
Another issue which I think is worth reporting
on is to update this Board on the work of

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:59.410
the Task and Finish Group on conflicts of
interest. You will recall that we launched

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:06.710
that earlier this year; we had a consultation
process that started in June, and after the

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:11.871
receipt of representations – I personally
read 900 pages of representations – there

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:20.530
was a strong response, and we have had a subsequent
meeting of the Task and Finish Group towards

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:24.900
the end of November where we have come to
provisional conclusions. There is a great

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:30.910
deal of detail still to be resolved, and we
are anxious to be able to produce a final

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:36.660
report which will come to this Board as soon
as possible, with an intent to be able to

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:42.720
have a consistent and coherent set of guidance
across the whole of the NHS, but also beyond,

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:48.400
to wherever the NHS pound is being spent.
We have had fantastic co‑operation by ABHI

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:54.410
and ABPI, so that we can try to intersect
what we are doing with what the industry are

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:59.890
trying to do, so that we have a commonality
of pattern overall. Also to stress that this

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:05.870
is not just about imposing new rules; this
is actually protecting individuals from the

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:12.220
imputation of improper motive through failure
to have declared and managed potentially conflicting

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:17.700
interests. So, it is a very important matter,
particularly as we move into an NHS which

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:23.980
is much more across silos rather than confined
into silos, and where new partnerships are

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:29.300
arising in a way which could give rise to
those imputations.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:34.310
The other issue that I would just like to
reflect upon is the 100,000 Genomes programme.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:39.880
As you are aware, I sat as a NED on the Board
of the Genomics England Limited company, wholly

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:44.640
owned by the Secretary of State. We had a
discussion earlier this week with the senior

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.630
ministers and the Secretary of State on progress.
I would say that there are two strands to

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:55.270
the programme. One is in relation to rare
diseases, where progress, from a faltering

00:07:55.270 --> 00:08:00.800
start, has now started to accelerate, and
where we are, I think, confidently on the

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:06.320
way to delivering the number of samples for
sequencing that we were originally programmed

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:10.840
to do.
Cancer is much more difficult. We are confident

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:15.889
that cancer will continue to grow as a major
component, but the difficulties are technological,

00:08:15.889 --> 00:08:21.650
they are scientific, they relate to the sampling,
but they also relate to being able to have

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:29.420
a reporting pipeline that will deliver clinically
valuable results back to clinicians in the

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:34.640
time that will allow them to use those results
in the treatment of patients. By the way,

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:42.140
this is cutting-edge stuff globally; this
is not merely a local difficulty. We will

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:46.570
be coming back to the Board in the New Year
with proposals around the re-procurement of

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:51.010
the genetics laboratories, and to build upon
the proposals that the Board approved earlier

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:55.290
this year towards personalised medicine within
the NHS.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:09:01.440
Finally, to report to the Board a decision
that was taken formally by the Chief Executive

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:06.760
and myself under standing orders following
a discussion at the Commissioning Committee

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:14.310
on 17th&nbsp;November, in the context of the continuing
issues that we have been having with Capita

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:20.710
in relation to primary care support services.
The decision that we have taken, and we are

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:25.770
now formally reporting to the Board, is that
we should extend the grace period for inclusion

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:31.540
on the Dental Performers List by a period
of eight weeks. I simply ask the Board to

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:37.120
note that, and that concludes the Chairman’s
Report. I am happy to take any questions or

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:37.980
comments.

